<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Greuthungi</h1><p class="st-meta">290 CE – 376 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The eastern Goths of the Ukrainian steppe, whose realm under Ermanaric was shattered by the Huns and set off the great migrations.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> moving south-east from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> reached the lands north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> in the 3rd century, the land <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> called Oium. By the late 3rd century the Greuthungi, the 'steppe people', had emerged east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a>, separate from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tervingi">Tervingi</a> to their west, and are linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernyakhov-culture">Chernyakhov culture</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> describes the Greuthungi as ruled by kings, and their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ermanaric">Ermanaric</a> was remembered as a mighty warrior whose dominion took in many peoples. Their society combined farming villages with a warrior aristocracy that raided and traded with the Roman world. Most remained pagan, though Gothic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> was spreading.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> attacked around 375, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ermanaric">Ermanaric</a> took his own life and his successor Vithimiris fell in battle. Many Greuthungi submitted to the Huns, while others under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alatheus">Alatheus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saphrax">Saphrax</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in 376 and helped crush the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Adrianople</a> in 378. Their descendants formed part of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/greuthungi.jpg" alt="Greuthungi" width="320" height="194"><figcaption>Image: en:User:Wiglaf, en:User:Dbachmann · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chernyakhov.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>290 CE – 376 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ermanaric, Vithimiris</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Gothic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gothic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ukraine</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Ermanaric, Oium</dd></dl></aside></div>

